<https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/claim/morphoeval-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting> a pk1:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Painter, Maini & Othmer (1999) Pomacanthus Turing-chemotaxis stripe doubling on a growing curved flank & Keller-Segel rippling with slime-trail tensor (Pomacanthus semicirculatus juvenile pigment stripes (model target) & Myxococcus xanthus rippling cell populations (specimen))",
        "Painter, Maini & Othmer (1999) Pomacanthus Turing-chemotaxis stripe doubling on a growing curved flank & Keller-Segel rippling with slime-trail tensor faithfully reproduces Pomacanthus semicirculatus juvenile pigment stripes (model target) & Myxococcus xanthus rippling cell populations (specimen) morphology" ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/math-law/painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-1>,
        <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/math-law/painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-2>,
        <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/math-law/painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-3>,
        <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/math-law/painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-4>,
        <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/math-law/painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-5>,
        <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/math-law/painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-6> ;
    dct:description "The simulation script(s) 1999-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting.py implement the cited source model as published and produce forms that match real Pomacanthus semicirculatus juvenile pigment stripes (model target) & Myxococcus xanthus rippling cell populations (specimen) specimens and the source authors' figures. A belief under audit, not a fact: its credibility is the weight of evidence (MorphoEval 0.1.0 overall 92.5/100, audit 2026-10-19-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-audit-v3) and the assessments." ;
    dct:subject <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/4d5512d/1999-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting.py> ;
    schema:about "Pomacanthus semicirculatus juvenile pigment stripes (model target) & Myxococcus xanthus rippling cell populations (specimen)" ;
    schema:additionalProperty "does_not_claim: Individual-cell C-signal contact-reversal mechanics or 3D fruiting-body stalk elevation",
        "does_not_claim: Reproduction of PNAS 1999 Fig. 4-5 anisotropic 2D growth (1.8e-4 / 2.2e-4) or Fig. 8 Lengyel-Epstein adult stripe-to-spot transition",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Computer-vision segmentation of the Myxococcus xanthus specimen (`chemotactic_pattern_specimen.jpg`, CC BY-SA 4.0): 4 fruiting-body spot aggregates and 362 rippling stripe spacings (74.10 um mean wavelength via the 500 um = 125 px scale bar)",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Generic Keller-Segel chemotaxis-logistic 1D/2D IMEX pseudospectral solvers (stationary s in {1, 2, 4, 8}, subcritical control chi = 3.0, growing-domain kymograph 1 -> 2 -> 4, 2D cubic stripes vs quadratic spots) and a slime-trail nematic tensor Q with mobility M = I + aQ tested by a chi-drop hysteresis protocol (grow at 6.2, hold at 4.0 / 4.4)",
        "reconstructed_in_script: PNAS 1999 Fig. 3 system integrated on a uniformly growing surface of revolution (1D line, cylinder K = 0, spindle K lambda_c^2 = 0.69) with a Laplace-Beltrami eigenbasis solver, fully implicit diffusion + Jacobian + 2r dilution, explicit clipped kinetics and chemotactic flux; stripe counts 3 -> 6 -> 12 on flat flanks (doubling scale ratio 2.03), 3 -> 6 -> 8 on the spindle; chi_0 sweep over two orders of magnitude",
        "reported_in_source: Balagam & Igoshin (2015) trail-following alignment of gliding M. xanthus cells",
        "reported_in_source: Keller & Segel (1970) J. Theor. Biol. 26:399-415 chemotactic flux cross-diffusion -div(n chi(c) grad(c)) and its linear instability; Crampin, Gaffney & Maini (1999) growing-domain dilution and mode doubling; Ermentrout (1991) stripe-vs-spot selection",
        "reported_in_source: Painter, Maini & Othmer (1999) PNAS 96(10):5549-5554 Eqs. (1)-(3): Kondo-Asai piecewise-linear Turing morphogens u, v with chemotactic iridophores n, chi(u) = chi_0/(k+u)^2, chi_0 < 0, on an exponentially growing domain L(t) = 11 exp(1e-4 t) (Fig. 3: 3-peak juvenile pattern; Fig. 1 / Kondo & Asai 1995: 3 -> 6 -> 12 stripes at 2, 6, 12 months)",
        "transfer_boundary: The Keller-Segel rippling model and slime-trail tensor are mapped to M. xanthus rippling ridges; the tensor is a continuum closure, not an agent-based trail model",
        "transfer_boundary: The PNAS 1999 iridophore model is compared against Kondo & Asai's stripe COUNTS (3, 6, 12) only; no Pomacanthus photograph is vendored, so curvature-dependent stripe geometry is not validated against a fish specimen" ;
    pk1:aristotelianEfficientCause "1. Chemical Turing / RD" ;
    pk1:aristotelianMegistonGenos "IV. Protista & Monera" ;
    pk1:aristotelianPoionForm "Stripes, Spots & Ocelli" ;
    pk1:aristotelianPosonLaw "Turing Wavenumber k_c" ;
    pk1:aristotelianSubalternClass "Slime Molds & Bacteria" ;
    pk1:biologicalGenus "Myxococcus" ;
    pk1:context "script(s): https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/4d5512d/1999-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting.py; output: docs/media/chemotactic_stripe_splitting_comparison.png; organism: Pomacanthus semicirculatus juvenile pigment stripes (model target) & Myxococcus xanthus rippling cell populations (specimen); checkpoint commit: https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/commit/4d5512d; environment: python 3.14.7, numpy 2.5.4, scipy 1.16.3, matplotlib 3.10.7, Pillow 12.0.0; spec_fingerprint: 61dda0c23fef2f0f55c7da992a8bddf50bfafc949a3445d76feebcc32dbff90f; artifact_sha256: 084966237a0de263e455d7da45279aad1290fd9d3bb43f31c32478fbcd15423e; input_dataset_sha256: {datasets/chemotactic_stripe_splitting/chemotactic_pattern_specimen.jpg=7b1780ffcb70f313..., datasets/chemotactic_stripe_splitting/chemotactic_stripe_splitting_reference.json=307220943134b2c2..., datasets/chemotactic_stripe_splitting/pomacanthus_stripe_doubling_reference.json=29e7fe6323be3efe..., datasets/chemotactic_stripe_splitting/scout_manifest.json=4b4e20a991f65c81...}; supersedes_audit_id: 2026-10-19-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-audit-v2; claim_boundary: [reported_in_source: Painter, Maini & Othmer (1999) PNAS 96(10):5549-5554 Eqs. (1)-(3): Kondo-Asai piecewise-linear Turing morphogens u, v with chemotactic iridophores n, chi(u) = chi_0/(k+u)^2, chi_0 < 0, on an exponentially growing domain L(t) = 11 exp(1e-4 t) (Fig. 3: 3-peak juvenile pattern; Fig. 1 / Kondo & Asai 1995: 3 -> 6 -> 12 stripes at 2, 6, 12 months) | Keller & Segel (1970) J. Theor. Biol. 26:399-415 chemotactic flux cross-diffusion -div(n chi(c) grad(c)) and its linear instability; Crampin, Gaffney & Maini (1999) growing-domain dilution and mode doubling; Ermentrout (1991) stripe-vs-spot selection | Balagam & Igoshin (2015) trail-following alignment of gliding M. xanthus cells; reconstructed_in_script: PNAS 1999 Fig. 3 system integrated on a uniformly growing surface of revolution (1D line, cylinder K = 0, spindle K lambda_c^2 = 0.69) with a Laplace-Beltrami eigenbasis solver, fully implicit diffusion + Jacobian + 2r dilution, explicit clipped kinetics and chemotactic flux; stripe counts 3 -> 6 -> 12 on flat flanks (doubling scale ratio 2.03), 3 -> 6 -> 8 on the spindle; chi_0 sweep over two orders of magnitude | Generic Keller-Segel chemotaxis-logistic 1D/2D IMEX pseudospectral solvers (stationary s in {1, 2, 4, 8}, subcritical control chi = 3.0, growing-domain kymograph 1 -> 2 -> 4, 2D cubic stripes vs quadratic spots) and a slime-trail nematic tensor Q with mobility M = I + aQ tested by a chi-drop hysteresis protocol (grow at 6.2, hold at 4.0 / 4.4) | Computer-vision segmentation of the Myxococcus xanthus specimen (`chemotactic_pattern_specimen.jpg`, CC BY-SA 4.0): 4 fruiting-body spot aggregates and 362 rippling stripe spacings (74.10 um mean wavelength via the 500 um = 125 px scale bar); transfer_boundary: The PNAS 1999 iridophore model is compared against Kondo & Asai's stripe COUNTS (3, 6, 12) only; no Pomacanthus photograph is vendored, so curvature-dependent stripe geometry is not validated against a fish specimen | The Keller-Segel rippling model and slime-trail tensor are mapped to M. xanthus rippling ridges; the tensor is a continuum closure, not an agent-based trail model; does_not_claim: Reproduction of PNAS 1999 Fig. 4-5 anisotropic 2D growth (1.8e-4 / 2.2e-4) or Fig. 8 Lengyel-Epstein adult stripe-to-spot transition | Individual-cell C-signal contact-reversal mechanics or 3D fruiting-body stalk elevation]" ;
    pk1:derivedFromPaper <https://doi.org/10.1006/bulm.1999.0131>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02458621>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-5193(70)90092-5>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.physd.2010.09.011>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1038/376765a0>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.96.10.5549>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1991.0100>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pcbi.1004474> ;
    pk1:hasAssessment <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-code-traceability>,
        <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-model-fidelity>,
        <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-morphometric-validation>,
