<https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-overall> a pk1:LLMClaimAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "Painter, Maini & Othmer (1999) Pomacanthus Turing-chemotaxis stripe doubling on a growing curved flank & Keller-Segel rippling with slime-trail tensor — MorphoEval overall 92.5/100" ;
    dct:date "2026-10-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:description "Pass 3 post-remediation evaluation of `1999-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting.py` (92.5/100, up from 91.3/100 in Pass 2). Both Pass 2 remediation items are closed: the actual PNAS 1999 Pomacanthus model (Kondo-Asai Turing morphogens + chemotactic iridophores, Fig. 3 parameters, linear analysis tr J = -0.01, det J = 0.005, h = 0.00458, k_c^2 = 3.2714, lambda_c = 3.4739, Neumann modes {5, 6, 7} in band, mode 6 -> 3 stripes) is integrated on a uniformly growing surface of revolution and reproduces Kondo & Asai's 3 -> 6 -> 12 stripe doubling on flat flanks (line and cylinder; 6 at s = 2.18, 12 at s = 4.44, ratio 2.03) while the curved spindle (K lambda_c^2 = 0.69) inserts stripes sequentially (3 -> 6 -> 8), and the Keller-Segel rippling cell flux is coupled to a slime-trail nematic order tensor whose Lean- proved threshold shift chi_c(m) = 4.22 lies inside the observed hysteresis bracket (4.0, 4.4) (ridge amplitude 0.46 with trails vs 0.16 without at chi = 4.4). The seed-paper title and the attribution of the Keller-Segel laws were corrected across script, datasets, Lean and KG YAML; the SHA-pinned `scout_manifest.json` still carries the wrong title and is disclosed. 13 reference-anchored measurements, 7 machine-checked Lean 4 theorems (0 sorrys), 9-panel figure and 3-row GIF. Residual defects: imposed 6.2/3.8 x/y anisotropy in the 2D KS stripe regime, spindle deviation from the doubling law, iridophore contrast 1.7x the quasi-static estimate, and no Pomacanthus specimen image. Overall 92.5/100 (faithfulness 92.3, reproducibility 92.9); rubric morphoeval 0.1.0; checkpoint commit 4d5512d; environment: python 3.14.7, numpy 2.5.4, scipy 1.16.3, matplotlib 3.10.7, Pillow 12.0.0. Dimension assessments: Model fidelity to source 95, Morphological realism / specimen match 90, Parameter expressivity 93, Morphometric validation 92, Source and data traceability 94, Code-to-model traceability 93, Reproducibility package 92. Output judged: docs/media/chemotactic_stripe_splitting_comparison.png: rendered output of 1999-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting.py at 4d5512d, image/png, 1522010 B, 2310×2044 px, artifact_sha256 084966237a0de263e455d7da45279aad1290fd9d3bb43f31c32478fbcd15423e (sha256 084966237a0de263e455d7da45279aad1290fd9d3bb43f31c32478fbcd15423e), spec_fingerprint 61dda0c23fef2f0f55c7da992a8bddf50bfafc949a3445d76feebcc32dbff90f. Dual provenance: spec_fingerprint: 61dda0c23fef2f0f55c7da992a8bddf50bfafc949a3445d76feebcc32dbff90f; artifact_sha256: 084966237a0de263e455d7da45279aad1290fd9d3bb43f31c32478fbcd15423e; input_dataset_sha256: {datasets/chemotactic_stripe_splitting/chemotactic_pattern_specimen.jpg=7b1780ffcb70f313..., datasets/chemotactic_stripe_splitting/chemotactic_stripe_splitting_reference.json=307220943134b2c2..., datasets/chemotactic_stripe_splitting/pomacanthus_stripe_doubling_reference.json=29e7fe6323be3efe..., datasets/chemotactic_stripe_splitting/scout_manifest.json=4b4e20a991f65c81...}. Supersedes audit: 2026-10-19-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-audit-v2. Claim boundary: reported_in_source: Painter, Maini & Othmer (1999) PNAS 96(10):5549-5554 Eqs. (1)-(3): Kondo-Asai piecewise-linear Turing morphogens u, v with chemotactic iridophores n, chi(u) = chi_0/(k+u)^2, chi_0 < 0, on an exponentially growing domain L(t) = 11 exp(1e-4 t) (Fig. 3: 3-peak juvenile pattern; Fig. 1 / Kondo & Asai 1995: 3 -> 6 -> 12 stripes at 2, 6, 12 months) | Keller & Segel (1970) J. Theor. Biol. 26:399-415 chemotactic flux cross-diffusion -div(n chi(c) grad(c)) and its linear instability; Crampin, Gaffney & Maini (1999) growing-domain dilution and mode doubling; Ermentrout (1991) stripe-vs-spot selection | Balagam & Igoshin (2015) trail-following alignment of gliding M. xanthus cells; reconstructed_in_script: PNAS 1999 Fig. 3 system integrated on a uniformly growing surface of revolution (1D line, cylinder K = 0, spindle K lambda_c^2 = 0.69) with a Laplace-Beltrami eigenbasis solver, fully implicit diffusion + Jacobian + 2r dilution, explicit clipped kinetics and chemotactic flux; stripe counts 3 -> 6 -> 12 on flat flanks (doubling scale ratio 2.03), 3 -> 6 -> 8 on the spindle; chi_0 sweep over two orders of magnitude | Generic Keller-Segel chemotaxis-logistic 1D/2D IMEX pseudospectral solvers (stationary s in {1, 2, 4, 8}, subcritical control chi = 3.0, growing-domain kymograph 1 -> 2 -> 4, 2D cubic stripes vs quadratic spots) and a slime-trail nematic tensor Q with mobility M = I + aQ tested by a chi-drop hysteresis protocol (grow at 6.2, hold at 4.0 / 4.4) | Computer-vision segmentation of the Myxococcus xanthus specimen (`chemotactic_pattern_specimen.jpg`, CC BY-SA 4.0): 4 fruiting-body spot aggregates and 362 rippling stripe spacings (74.10 um mean wavelength via the 500 um = 125 px scale bar); transfer_boundary: The PNAS 1999 iridophore model is compared against Kondo & Asai's stripe COUNTS (3, 6, 12) only; no Pomacanthus photograph is vendored, so curvature-dependent stripe geometry is not validated against a fish specimen | The Keller-Segel rippling model and slime-trail tensor are mapped to M. xanthus rippling ridges; the tensor is a continuum closure, not an agent-based trail model; does_not_claim: Reproduction of PNAS 1999 Fig. 4-5 anisotropic 2D growth (1.8e-4 / 2.2e-4) or Fig. 8 Lengyel-Epstein adult stripe-to-spot transition | Individual-cell C-signal contact-reversal mechanics or 3D fruiting-body stalk elevation." ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/concept/morphoeval-rubric-0.1.0> ;
    pk1:assessedBy <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/model/gemini-4-argon> ;
    pk1:assessmentType "morphoeval/0.1.0/overall" ;
    pk1:evidenceAgainst "remediation needed: Iridophore contrast is 1.73x the quasi-static flux-balance estimate (measurement 10, L1253-1262) because n is advected during the 6 -> 12 split at s = 4.44: add a relaxation-time measurement (hold the domain after the last split until contrast settles) so the Boltzmann estimate becomes a true bound.",
        "remediation needed: The spindle profile reaches 8 stripes at s = 5.2 against the Kondo-Asai 12 (measurement 9, L1243-1252): sweep `taper` and `C_0` in `_surface_profile` (L616-638) to quantify the critical curvature K lambda_c^2 at which synchronous doubling breaks down, and add a Lean statement bounding the metric-gradient term rho'/rho relative to k_c.",
        "remediation needed: `simulate_2d_stripe_vs_spot` (1999-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting.py L466-541) still obtains 2D stripes by imposing a 6.2/3.8 x/y chemotactic anisotropy (docstring L478-483): replace it with the slime-trail mobility tensor of `simulate_slime_trail_ks` (L891-997) so stripe orientation emerges from Q, and compare ridge spacing against the 74.1 um specimen wavelength.",
        "remediation needed: `simulate_pomacanthus_growing_surface` (L703-856) uses uniform isotropic growth; expose the PNAS 1999 Fig. 4 anisotropic rostro-caudal / dorso-ventral growth rates (1.8e-4 / 2.2e-4, stored in `pomacanthus_stripe_doubling_reference.json` `fig4_parameters_2d`) as separate metric scale factors s_X(t), s_theta(t) and reproduce the Fig. 5 stripe break-up on the 3.8 x 1.9 domain.",
        "remediation needed: `verify_dataset_integrity` (L137-156) pins `scout_manifest.json` (SHA 4b4e20a9...) which still carries the non-existent title 'Stripe and spotilihood in chemotactic pattern formation': issue a `scout_manifest_v2.json` with the corrected title and PMID, pin it, and mark the v1 manifest as superseded in `chemotactic_stripe_splitting_reference.json` (L44-47)." ;
    pk1:hasScore 9.25e-01 ;
    pk1:hasVerdict "faithful" .

<https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-parameter-expressivity> a pk1:LLMClaimAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "Painter, Maini & Othmer (1999) Pomacanthus Turing-chemotaxis stripe doubling on a growing curved flank & Keller-Segel rippling with slime-trail tensor — Parameter expressivity: 93/100" ;
    dct:date "2026-10-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:description "Beyond the Pass 2 regimes (s in {1, 2, 4, 8}, chi in {0, 3, 4.5, 6}, 2D stripes vs spots), Pass 3 adds three surface profiles (`_surface_profile`, L616-638: line, cylinder, spindle with tunable taper), a chi_0 sweep over two orders of magnitude (`pomacanthus_chi0_sweep`, L857-879: contrast 0.018 / 0.201 / 5.86), and a 2 x 2 trail-strength x hold-chi hysteresis grid (`evaluate_slime_trail_hysteresis`, L998-1022). Deduction: anisotropic RC/DV growth rates (PNAS 1999 Fig. 4) are stored in the dataset but not exposed as a solver parameter." ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/concept/morphoeval-0.1.0-parameter-expressivity> ;
    pk1:assessedBy <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/model/gemini-4-argon> ;
    pk1:assessmentType "morphoeval/0.1.0/parameter-expressivity" ;
    pk1:hasEvidence <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/evidence/morphoeval-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-parameter-expressivity-1>,
        <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/evidence/morphoeval-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-parameter-expressivity-2>,
        <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/evidence/morphoeval-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-parameter-expressivity-3> ;
    pk1:hasScore 9.3e-01 ;
    pk1:hasVerdict "faithful" .

<https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-repro-package> a pk1:LLMClaimAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "Painter, Maini & Othmer (1999) Pomacanthus Turing-chemotaxis stripe doubling on a growing curved flank & Keller-Segel rippling with slime-trail tensor — Reproducibility package: 92/100" ;
    dct:date "2026-10-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:description "Deterministic RNG (`seed=1999`), memoised simulations (`_memo`, L119-123) so the self-test and render share runs, and an extended `--self-test` (`run_self_test`, L1744-1849) that checks 4 SHA-256 digests, the corrected title, Turing quantities to 1e-12, exact stripe sequences 3 -> 6 -> 12 on line and cylinder, bitwise determinism of the surface solver (`atol=0.0`), contrast ratio bounds, chi_0 monotonicity, Law-6 threshold inside the hysteresis bracket and 13 measurements. Deductions: default render (92 s) and self-test (108 s) are within the 3-minute budget but the cylinder/spindle runs (~30 s each at nx = 256) make the suite slow, and nx = 128 silently degrades the second doubling (documented in the solver docstring L729-733) so resolution is a hidden correctness parameter." ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/concept/morphoeval-0.1.0-repro-package> ;
    pk1:assessedBy <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/model/gemini-4-argon> ;
    pk1:assessmentType "morphoeval/0.1.0/repro-package" ;
    pk1:evidenceAgainst "1999-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting.py L729–L733: Solver docstring: nx = 256 required, nx = 128 degrades the second doubling to 6 -> 8 (resolution-sensitive result) <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/4d5512d/1999-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting.py#L729-L733>" ;
    pk1:hasEvidence <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/evidence/morphoeval-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-repro-package-1>,
        <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/evidence/morphoeval-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-repro-package-2> ;
    pk1:hasScore 9.2e-01 ;
    pk1:hasVerdict "faithful" .

<https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/assessment/morphoeval-painter-chemotactic-stripe-splitting-source-traceability> a pk1:LLMClaimAssessment ;
